#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126211 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "767cf28e-15b6-599c-8613-52500710d20b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129235 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129235 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129235 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129235 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129235 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129235 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129235 #edb/ref 1039 "Classifying a Sum or Difference of Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129235 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186129054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129235 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bbf0b9c3-84ba-51c8-bb7e-a45a441834ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129236 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129236 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129236 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129236 #edb/ref 1039 "Classifying a Product or Quotient of Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129236 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186129055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129236 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "36b04804-a1f3-57de-b966-65074210fc6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129373 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129373 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129373 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129373 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129373 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129373 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129373 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129373 #edb/ref 1045 "Sums and Products of Rational and Irrational Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129373 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186130150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129373 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129373 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "15f10ca2-71ff-5122-8f23-034d870b20ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129451 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129451 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133430" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129451 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129451 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $a$ is an **irrational** number, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $17$ is rational\n\nII. $17a$ is rational\n\nIII. $a\\cdot 17$ is irrational" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129451 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "18fad891-cd2e-5b5f-9e81-fba725a5c444" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129451 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that: \n\n- the product of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}\\times \\text{rational}=\\text{rational}\n$$\n\n- the product of a nonzero rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}\\times \\text{irrational}=\\text{irrational}\n$$\n\n- the product of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n- Statement II is false. The product of a rational number ($17$) and an irrational number ($a$) is irrational.\n\n- Statement III is true. We know from part II that $17a$ is irrational. And since multiplication is commutative, we conclude that $a\\cdot 17$ is irrational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130150 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130150 #edb/ref 1061 36260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130150 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "37af3d14-d0da-5833-8cc2-8d34a27c4558" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130150 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130151 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130151 #edb/ref 1061 36261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130151 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8ae24fd7-3348-5c2c-97a8-bf4a352b05a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130151 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130152 #edb/ref 1061 36263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130152 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6e1aa31d-da89-5630-8461-cb2becfe2266" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130152 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130153 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130153 #edb/ref 1061 36266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130153 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f1b0c4be-3561-5047-b474-fc789baf401e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130153 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130161 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130161 #edb/ref 1061 36625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130161 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "120df569-6a65-5f8d-9c7d-5fcfadd9b271" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130161 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130162 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130162 #edb/ref 1061 36626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130162 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d13633da-c1a6-540e-a940-eff75190b892" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130162 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130432 #edb/ref 1064 "When adding numbers, it's usually possible to say whether the sum is rational or irrational.\n\nWe have three possible cases:\n\n- The sum of two rational numbers is *always* rational. Rational numbers can be represented as fractions of the form $\\dfrac ab$ (where $a$ and $b$ are integers), and adding two of these fractions always produces another fraction.\n- The sum of a rational and an irrational number is *always* irrational. To demonstrate, let's consider the following example:\n$\\sqrt 2 + \\dfrac12$\nIt's easy to see that this is irrational. Writing down the sum in decimal form, we get\n$1.414213562… + 0.5 = 1.914213562…$\nwhich is irrational since the numbers after the decimal point extend forever with no regular pattern.\n- The sum of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational! For example, the following sum is irrational:\n$\\sqrt 2 + \\sqrt 3 = 3.146\\,264\\,37\\ldots$\nHowever, the following sum is rational:\n$\\sqrt 2 + (-\\sqrt 2) = 0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130432 #edb/ref 1065 5941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130432 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1b2b00f9-12fd-5755-9340-1a9477f61cdc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130432 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130433 #edb/ref 1064 "When multiplying real numbers, it's usually possible to say whether the product is rational or irrational.\n\nWe have three possible cases:\n\n- The product of two rational numbers is *always* rational since the result must be a fraction of the form $\\dfrac ab$, where $a$ and $b$ are integers.\n- The product of a *nonzero* rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational. To demonstrate, let's consider the following example:\n$2\\cdot \\sqrt 2$\nIt's easy to see that this is irrational. Writing down the product in decimal form, we get\n$2 \\cdot 1.414213562… = 2.828427125…$\nwhich is irrational since the numbers after the decimal point extend forever with no regular pattern.\n- The product of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational! For example, the following product is irrational:\n$\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{3}|= \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 3}; = \\sqrt{6}$\nHowever, the following product is rational:\n$\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2}|= \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 2}; = \\sqrt{2^{2}}; = 2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130433 #edb/ref 1065 5942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130433 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "74425e69-1e7a-5c1c-b1ac-2aec2e2ad1df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130433 #edb/ref 1067 "Products of Rational and Irrational Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130437 #edb/ref 1064 "To summarize:\n\n- the sum (or difference) of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n$\\textrm{rational} + \\textrm{rational} = \\textrm{rational}$\n- the sum (or difference) of a rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n$\\textrm{rational} + \\textrm{irrational} = \\textrm{irrational}$\n- the sum (or difference) of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130437 #edb/ref 1065 5998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130437 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "df9f71cf-81c8-546c-99f3-cca45192f745" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130437 #edb/ref 1067 "A Summary" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130438 #edb/ref 1064 "To summarize:\n\n- the product (or quotient) of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n$\\textrm{rational} \\times \\textrm{rational} = \\textrm{rational}$\n- the product (or quotient) of a nonzero rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n$\\textrm{rational} \\times \\textrm{irrational} = \\textrm{irrational}$\n- the product (or quotient) of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nFinally, it's worth pointing out that if $a$ is irrational, then the second rule implies that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac1a\n$$\n\nis also irrational." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130438 #edb/ref 1065 5999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130438 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "45f6a1bf-6d91-5130-8aa5-a2d5863e2654" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130438 #edb/ref 1067 "A Summary" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186517490 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186518234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186517490 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186518245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186517490 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186518262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186517490 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186517490 #edb/ref 1045 "Sums and Products of Rational and Irrational Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186517490 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186518234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186517490 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186517490 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186517490 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "a2a8669c-ee96-56c4-bb5a-a6fd2bdf735e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186517510 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186517510 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186517510 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0b1658d7-fb9d-5945-a721-88c70ad2282a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186517633 #edb/ref 1080 "None of those listed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186517633 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186517633 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4afb3715-3f88-56d5-b275-bc81df1d0871" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186517637 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186517637 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186517637 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4bc3bc0f-8609-527a-aef7-251c33edf7a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186517654 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186517654 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186517654 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "592eee93-53ea-5ef8-a416-d9e903eec0f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186517681 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186517681 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186517681 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6968ee8d-91bd-5ab9-bb4d-43e24c86d97a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186517702 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186517702 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186517702 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6f10f982-4e9f-520e-8314-481be60