592186518059 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518059 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518059 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518059 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518059 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186517637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518059 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bbb07e29-0dfc-5f19-8c86-10222960800a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518120 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518120 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133670" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518120 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518120 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $a$ is an **irrational** number, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\frac{1}{7}$ is irrational\n\nII. $\\frac{1}{7}+a$ is always irrational\n\nIII. $a-\\frac{1}{7}$ is sometimes rational and sometimes irrational" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518120 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b569a4a-634a-5c5d-8e62-c4297125cda8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518120 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that: \n\n- the sum of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}+\\text{rational}=\\text{rational}\n$$\n\n- the sum of a rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}+\\text{irrational}=\\text{irrational}\n$$\n\n- the sum of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\frac{1}{7}$ is rational and $a$ is irrational, their sum is always an irrational number.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\frac{1}{7}$ is rational and $a$ is irrational, their difference is always an irrational number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518210 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518210 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133671" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518210 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518210 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $a$ is an **irrational** number, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $-\\frac{1}{{4}^{12}}$ is irrational\n\nII. $a-\\frac{1}{{4}^{12}}$ is always rational\n\nIII. $a+\\frac{1}{{4}^{12}}$ is sometimes rational and sometimes irrational" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518210 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e5ec21fc-d95b-5a6e-8483-328c912290e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518210 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that: \n\n- the sum of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}+\\text{rational}=\\text{rational}\n$$\n\n- the sum of a rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}+\\text{irrational}=\\text{irrational}\n$$\n\n- the sum of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $-\\frac{1}{{4}^{12}}$ is rational and $a$ is irrational, their sum or difference is always an irrational number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518234 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186518262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518234 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2e9d24d0-afef-510e-9520-1823a6868cfd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518234 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518245 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3d764519-dc05-5945-a5e0-05d704da56e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518245 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186518210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518262 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186518245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518262 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "777d28dd-d1bc-5505-b0d0-f28dac65000a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518262 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186518120 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
